Ladybird bounds an HTML element’s SVG filter target by what the element
and its descendants paint: their border boxes, inline boxes included,
and outer shadows, each cut by its containing blocks’ overflow clips
along the axes they clip and moved by their transforms, in that order.
Chrome 157, Firefox 151 and WebKit 26.5 all draw a box’s shadow, a
positioned box that escapes an “overflow: hidden” box, and an inline box
moved out past the start edge whole under an identity filter, verified
at runtime with the new tests. Chrome and Firefox also draw the child of
a rotated box that clips only its x-axis whole, and WebKit cuts it.

The other engines bound the target by what the box paints: Blink in
PaintLayer::UpdateFilterReferenceBox(), by visual overflow, and Gecko in
SVGIntegrationUtils::GetSVGBBoxForNonSVGFrame(), by ink overflow.
Ladybird’s own ink overflow can’t stand in for those, since it always
reserves room for a focus outline, which would grow every box.
